== 1. Presenting the Bait and Hiding the Hook

Now the second thing that I am to show you is his various devices. And here I will first show you the various devices that he has to draw the soul to sin. I will give these twelve as instances, which may call for our most serious consideration.

His first device to draw the soul to sin is to present the bait and hide the hook; to present the golden cup and hide the poison; to present the sweetness, the pleasure, and the profit that may flow in on the soul by yielding to sin, and to hide from the soul the wrath and misery that will certainly follow the committing of sin. By this device he took our first parents. Gen 3:4, 5: “And the serpent said to the woman, ‘You#super[+] will not surely die. For God#footnote[So, to bring back Dr. Taylor the martyr, they promised him not only his pardon, but a bishopric. Foxe, #emph[Acts and Monuments], folio 1386. #emph[Inest peccatum cum delectaris: regnat si consenseris.] \[Sin is present when you delight in it; it reigns if you consent to it.\] Augustine, on Ps 50.] knows that in the day you#super[+] eat of it, your eyes will be opened and you will be like God, knowing good and evil.’” Your#super[+] eyes will be opened, and you will be like God: here is the bait, the sweetness, the pleasure, the profit. Oh, but he hides the hook, the shame, the wrath, and the loss that would certainly follow! There is an opening of the eyes of the mind to contemplation and joy, and there is an opening of the eyes of the body to shame and confusion. He promises them the former, but intends the latter, and so cheats them, giving them an apple in exchange for Paradise, as he deals with thousands nowadays. Satan easily deceives us with fallacies, and then by his golden baits leads us on and leaves us in a fool’s paradise. He promises the soul honor, pleasure, and profit, etc., but pays the soul with the greatest contempt, shame, and loss that can be. By a golden bait he labored to catch Christ#footnote[This world shall at last be burned for a witch, says one. #emph[Multi amando res noxias sunt miseri, habendo miseriores.] Augustine, on Ps 26. Many are miserable by loving hurtful things, but they are more miserable by having them. Men need to pray with Bernard, #emph[Da domine ut sic possideamus temporalia ut non perdamus aeterna.] Grant us, Lord, that we may so share in temporal happiness that we may not lose eternal.] in Matt 4:8, 9. He shows him the beauty and the splendor of a bewitching world, which no doubt would have taken many a carnal heart; but here the devil’s fire fell on wet tinder, and so did not catch. These tempting objects did not win upon his affections at all, nor dazzle his eyes. Yet many have died eternally of the wound of the eye, and fallen forever by this vile strumpet the world, who by laying out her two fair breasts of profit and pleasure has wounded their souls and cast them down into utter perdition. By the glitter of her pomp and preferment she has slain millions, like the serpent Scytale, which, when she cannot overtake fleeing travelers, stuns and dazes them with her beautiful colors, so that they have no power to go on their way until she has stung them to death. Adversity has slain her thousand, but prosperity her ten thousand.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of the devil are these.]

First, keep at the greatest distance from sin, and from playing with the golden bait that Satan holds out to catch you. For this you have Rom 12:9: “Abhor what is evil; cling to what is good.” When we meet with anything extremely evil and contrary to us, nature abhors it, and draws back as far as it can#footnote[ἀποστυγοῦντες. The simple verb conveys extreme detestation, which is heightened by the compound. Chrysostom.] from it. The Greek word that is there rendered #emph[abhor] is very expressive: it signifies to hate it as hell itself, to hate it with horror. Anselm used to say that if he should see the shame of sin on the one hand and the pains of hell on the other, and must of necessity choose one, he would rather be thrust into hell without sin than go into heaven with sin; so great was his hatred and detestation of sin. It is our wisest and our safest#footnote[Prov 5:8. 1 Thess 5:22.] course to stand at the furthest distance from sin; not to go near the house of the harlot, but to flee “from every appearance of evil.” The best way to avoid falling into the pit is to keep at the#footnote[It was a divine saying of a heathen that if there were no God to punish him, no devil to torment him, no hell to burn him, no man to see him, yet he would not sin, because of the ugliness and filthiness of sin and the grief of his own conscience. Seneca.] greatest distance from it. He who is so bold as to attempt to dance on the brink of the pit may find by woeful experience that it is a righteous thing with God that he should fall into the pit. Joseph keeps at a distance from sin, and from playing with Satan’s golden baits, and stands. David draws near and plays with the bait, and falls, and swallows bait and hook with a vengeance. David comes near the snare and is taken in it, to the breaking of his bones, the wounding of his conscience, and the loss of his God.

Sin is a plague, indeed the greatest and the most infectious plague in the world; and yet, ah, how few there are who tremble at it, who keep at a distance from it! 1 Cor 5:6: “Do you#super[+] not know that a little leaven works through the whole batch of dough?” As soon as one sin had seized on Adam’s heart, all sin entered into his soul and overspread#footnote[Sin is like those diseases that physicians call #emph[Corruptio totius substantiae] \[corruption of the whole substance\].] it. How Adam’s one sin has spread over all mankind! Rom 5:12: “Therefore, just as sin entered the world through one man, and death through sin, so also death was passed on to all men, because all sinned.” Ah, how the father’s sin infects the child, the husband’s the wife, the master’s the servant! The sin that is in one man’s heart is able to infect a whole world, it is of such a spreading and infectious nature. The story of the Italian who first made his enemy deny God and then stabbed him, and so murdered both body and soul at once, shows the utter malignity of sin. And oh, that what has been said on this head may prevail with you to stand at a distance from sin!

The second #emph[remedy] is to consider#footnote[When the golden bait is cast out to catch us, we must say as Demosthenes the orator did of the beautiful Lais, when an excessive sum of money was asked of him to see her: “I will not buy repentance so dear; I am not so poor a merchant as to sell eternal things for temporal ones.”] that sin is only a bitter-sweet. The seeming sweetness that is in sin will quickly vanish, and lasting shame, sorrow, horror, and terror will come in its place. Job 20:12, 13, 14: “Though evil is sweet in his mouth and he conceals it under his tongue, though he cannot bear to let it go and keeps it in his mouth, yet in his stomach his food sours into the venom of asps within him.” Forbidden profits and pleasures are most pleasing to vain men, who count madness mirth, etc. Many long to be meddling with the murdering morsels of sin, which do not nourish, but tear and consume the belly, the soul that receives them. Many eat on earth what they digest in hell; sin’s murdering morsels will deceive those who devour them. Adam’s#footnote[If intemperance could give more pleasure than temperance, Heliogabalus would have been happier than Adam in Paradise.] apple was a bitter-sweet; Esau’s stew was a bitter-sweet; the Israelites’ quails a bitter-sweet, Jonathan’s honey a bitter-sweet, and Adonijah’s dainties a bitter-sweet. After the meal is ended comes the reckoning. Men must not think to dance and dine with the devil, and then to sit at supper with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven; to feed on the poison of asps, and yet think that the viper’s tongue will not slay them. When the asp stings a man, it first tickles him so that it makes him laugh, until the poison little by#footnote[Plutarch.] little gets to the heart, and then it pains him more than ever it delighted him. So does sin: it may please a little at first, but it will pain the soul with a vengeance at last. Indeed, if there were the least real delight in sin, there could be no perfect hell, where men will be most perfectly tormented with their sin.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that sin will usher in the greatest and the#footnote[Isa 59:2. Ps 51:12. Isa 59:8. 2 Chr 15:3, 4. Jer 17:18.] saddest losses that can befall our souls. It will usher in the loss of that divine favor which is better than life, and the loss of that joy which is inexpressible and full of glory, and the loss of that peace which surpasses understanding, and the loss of those divine influences by which the soul has been refreshed, enlivened,#footnote[Jer 5:25.] raised, strengthened, and gladdened, and the loss of many desirable outward mercies which the soul might otherwise have enjoyed. It was a sound and wholesome reply of an English captain at the loss of Calais. When a proud Frenchman scornfully asked, “When will you fetch Calais back?” he replied,#footnote[Quando peccata vestra, erunt nostris graviora.] “When your sins weigh down ours.” Ah, England! My constant prayer for you is that you may not sin away your mercies into the hands of those who cannot call mercy mercy, and who would rejoice in nothing more than to see your sorrow and misery, and to see the hand that has clothed you with much mercy and glory make you naked.

The fourth remedy against this device#footnote[In Sardis there grew a herb called #emph[Apium Sardis] that would make a man lie laughing when he was deathly sick; such is the working of sin.] of Satan is to consider seriously that sin is of a very deceitful and bewitching nature. Sin comes from the greatest deceiver; it is a child of his own begetting, it is the ground of all the deceit in the world, and it is in its own nature exceedingly deceitful. Heb 3:13: “But exhort one another daily, as long as it is called today, so that none of you#super[+] may be hardened by sin’s deceitfulness.” It will kiss the soul, and make fair promises to the soul, and yet betray the soul forever. Like Delilah, it will smile on us so that it may betray us into the hands of the devil, as she betrayed Samson into the hands of the#footnote[Prov 5:22, 23.] Philistines. Sin gives Satan a power over us, and an advantage to accuse us, and to lay claim to us as those who wear his badge. It is of a very bewitching nature: it so bewitches the soul where it is on the throne that the soul cannot leave it, though it perishes eternally by it. Sin so bewitches the soul that it makes#footnote[This led Chrysostom to say, when Eudoxia the empress threatened him, “Go tell her (#emph[Nil nisi peccatum timeo]) I fear nothing but sin.”] the soul call evil good and good evil, bitter sweet and sweet bitter, light darkness and darkness light. And a soul thus bewitched with sin will hold out to the death, at sword’s point with God. Let God strike, and wound, and cut to the very bone, yet the bewitched soul does not care, does not fear, but will keep on in a course of wickedness, as you may see in Pharaoh, Balaam, and Judas. Tell the bewitched soul that sin is a viper that will certainly kill when it is not killed, that sin often kills secretly, unnoticed, eternally; yet the bewitched soul cannot and will not cease from sin. When the physicians told#footnote[#emph[Vale lumen amicum.] Ambrose.] Theotimus that unless he abstained from drunkenness and uncleanness, etc., he would lose his eyes, his heart was so bewitched with his sins that he answered, “Then farewell, sweet light!” He would rather lose his eyes than leave his sins. So a man bewitched with sin would rather lose God, Christ, heaven, and his own soul than part with his sin. Oh, therefore, forever take heed of playing or nibbling at Satan’s golden baits!
